ne else  did too I'm sure at times that the staff  experienced me as completely impossible  I was often beyond reach after I've been  at pi/4 about six months I made a  significant suicide attempt while I was  home on a weekend pass after that I  didn't go home even to visit for close  to a year in July 1964 after almost a  year at p.i I was assigned to a new  psychiatry resident Stanley Heller was  someone I liked and respected I thought  he was handsome and smart and he talked  to me I felt that he took me seriously  he didn't talk down to me or lecture me  his sense of humor matched mine when I  made stupid puns he laughed when I told  him that evil garbage stank and should  be eliminated he told me he knew someone  who looked for treasures in garbage one  person's stink is another's perfume dr.  Heller noted astonishing me humiliation  and shame could be interrupted if only  briefly by my admiration for his  cleverness  I continue to act out in  self-destructive ways but his response  to me was direct and real what a pain in  the neck he said once after I battered  my head yet again I had to laugh at  myself in the spark of a good joke we  were peers we connected he was not  blaming or intrusive with my with  interpretations of my motives instead he  was straightforward and looked at what I  had done and asked why now how come what  are you doing as if he thought there  were understandable reasons for my  actions that we could figure out  together when I did something  particularly dangerous he showed that he  cared that I had been hurt and he  expressed his anger in a way that showed  showed me that he worried about me I had  been unlucky and that I was misdiagnosed  and the treatment traumatized me but I  was lucky that in the late 1960s along  with medication and shock treatment in  the teaching hospitals there was an  enormous investment in psychotherapy as  treatment so eventually I was able to  connect with dr. Stanley Heller and to  begin to find myself a colleague asked  me if I could explain how as a patient I  differentiated the psychiatric residence  I respected from those I did not I think  I can safely speak for most of us at the  time when I say that I could sense which  residents were afraid of patients and  which were comfortable with themselves  and with patients as fellow humans  I could tell who was speaking past me  mouthing empty phrases without noticing  if I was listening closed and aloof  afraid of being contaminated if touched  the finest therapists understood their  work to be a collaboration with each  patient they weren't afraid to journey  together through an unknown and possibly  dangerous land subjectively when I felt  understood and respected that is taken  seriously which was infrequent I was  grateful and in return I gave that  person  doctor or nurse enormous respect they  also got my loyalty I gave them room to  be imperfect in subsequent interactions  as long as they were honest and real  feeling understood is the essence of  connection and ultimately it's this  connection that carries healing an  example of this ability to connect comes  from a set of interviews I had with  Harold Searles in late night in late  1964 Harold Searles was an  internationally recognized psychiatrist  considered a master clinician who  pioneered counter transference and  treating psychotic and borderline  patients at the time he was the clinical  supervisor for my psychiatrist Stan  Heller in the first interview Jones made  references to my shrinking self effacing  demeanor and behavior on the ward and  told me I acted like I wanted to be a  saint I don't remember that interview  beyond my thinking that he was a nun  comprehending jerk but there was a  second interview and that one I've  always remembered and credited and I  knew this even at the time somehow with  changing my life in the second interview  Harold Searles referred to my interest  in being perfect the Saint thing and  then he remarked that I appeared to him  like a frightened young fawn deer in the  headlights my frozen innocence and fear  made him feel like a dirty old man he  told me and this made him feel so bad  about himself that he wanted to kill  himself one remarkable feature of this  exchange as I would later understand is  that unconsciously I had communicated  and he had received the essence of the  truth that was unknown to me at the time  my paralyzing fear was indeed connected  with a dirty old man and was driving my  obsession with suicide a transcript of  this interaction might appear bizarre or  quite inappropriate  however for me although I couldn't  explain it consciously  dr. Searles made contact I felt  connected and understood the life  changing aspect was the idea that I  could affect the feelings of someone  else especially in this case a respected  Authority I had caused him to feel  something for someone who felt as if she  floated alone in the universe unreal and  completely without influence this was a  new concept that night following the  interview I told the other patients  gathered at our informal evening coffee  discussion group what had happened he  said I made him feel guilty like a dirty  old man like I was some kind of Saint  can you imagine hell yes said one you're  always doing stuff for other people  yeah chimed in another you do that right  here when someone sneezes who's the  first of governing for a tissue it's  sickening don't you realize you make us  feel like we're not as good as you do  when you do that all the time like you  have to be better yeah I feel useless  sometimes like you have to get there  first to help one after another my  friends and even some patients I hardly  knew gave examples of my oppressive  helpfulness I'm still a little like that  at first I felt bewildered but I got it  I think my awareness that I had an  effect on other people began my healing  I experienced myself differently I  observed myself interacting working with  dr. Heller who had laid the groundwork  and whom I trusted as my guide I was on  my way after I turned this important  corner  what registered with me were the times  when dr. Heller's stepped beyond his  white coat if you will they really wore  white coats then and treated me as if I  were a reasonable person not a mental  patient I suppose as translator I'd say  that when I refer to being seen as a  mental patient  it meant I felt I was being treated as a  less than human object but dr. Heller  spoke to me as one whole person to  another and he noticed the healthy part  of me  gradually I could tell he believed in my  strength he believed that I could make  it make a life for myself outside of  institutions I knew he was showing me  how healthy people interact toward the  end of that second year at p.i when the  July rotations were approaching I  realized that I might possibly remain  dr. Heller's patient if I got my  behavior under control  similarly I understood that if I failed  to control myself  I'd have no chance to continue treatment  with him I managed to succeed well  enough and I remained his patient  through the rest of his residency a year  later I knew I had to be well enough to  be discharged in order to continue to  work with him after he left bi and  opened his private practice I moved out  of Pi the day before my 23rd birthday  about six weeks before July 1st dr.  Heller and I continued to work together  for the next five years I was employed  at several retail and then clerical jobs  during the year I was leaving PI as a  patient in a state mental hospital I was  eligible for state services and through  the Department of vocational  rehabilitation  Billiton I was sent for psych testing  although still diagnosed as  schizophrenic I would I was considered  bright enough for college so they sent  me to school that's where really the  good old days the next year I began  college at the school of general studies  at Columbia at school I met the man who  was to become my husband we fell in love  after a courtship developed from our  intellectual kinship we reveled in  discussions about our sociology class  and the concepts and theories we were  learning there two years later I married  him and moved to New Haven where he was  going to attend graduate school at Yale  in sociology Yale College was accepting  transfer students for the upper classes  as part of its first year as a  co-educational college of course I was  rejected as a transfer student however  as a Yale spouse I was allowed to attend  classes as a special student I spent  most of my junior year walking around  with a giant chip on my shoulder I  should have been grateful and in many  ways I was but all I saw were beautiful  young women whose applications had not  been rejected and I was envious  Yale's transition to co-education was  difficult and many women left  a year later Yale reopened admissions  for transfer students I applied again  and this time I was accepted to  celebrate dr. Hillier brought a half  bottle of champagne to our session and  we toasted the momentous occasion  he also got he was afraid to tell his  supervisors it was a marvelous  affirmation of my assertiveness and  adult accomplishment in 1971 I graduated  with the first class of Yale women summa  [ __ ] laude Phi Beta Kappa with highest  honors in sociology this intern opened  further doors for me by encouraging  people to take me seriously to give me a  chance when other aspects of my history  might have made them shy away from  investing in me I was determined to  become a psychologist myself and my  journey to achieve that goal also  involved Sara serendipity good fortune  and having those who believed in me and  sheer grit and determination on my part  stubbornness actually I applied and was  initially rejected for a graduate  psychology program and then for the  clinical psychology internship  essentially I proved absolutely  determined I refused to stop trying  until various directors in various  programs said okay if she wants that  this much why not let her and then let  my not let her do it and then I did it  for the first two years after I was  married and living in New Haven I  commuted to New York City to see dr.  Heller then I knew it was time for me to  learn more about how to be a woman and  with his blessing I transferred to a  female psychiatrist in New Haven she and  I worked together for the next ten years  while I cried I attended graduate school  did my internship had my babies and  carried out my dissertation in 1981  after twenty-one years of therapy I  stopped three months prior to my last  appointment I had a transformative  experience while on the way to my  session I suddenly found myself  intensely vividly alive and present the  depersonalization vanished I was real it  was as if the world suddenly took on  color when before I'd known only  shades of grey when I terminated three  months later for the first time in my  life I felt I was truly on my own at the  same time I knew that my therapist was  still there for me if I needed her that  fall I finished my dissertation and was  hired by a child psychiatrist expanding  his clinic I joined him and his partner  the psychologist the late psychologist  Daniel Miller who became my supervisor  and mentor I've worked full-time ever  since  first with that practice and since 1990  on my own I spent those years learning  from my patients and always trying to  understand what it is that leads to  people getting well a few years ago 40  years after I had been first admitted to  the hospital I seemed to be successful  personally and professionally I proven  to myself that I'd made it but I  continued to live with what felt like  the shameful secret of my hospital  experience and the reality that I still  had no memories of my childhood most of  my first 20 years I avoided talking  about my youth generalizing and  confabulating when necessary from facts  I've been told or read about myself  only a few people knew about my history  and psychiatric hospitals or my  extensive psychotherapy after that my  children were grown and on their own I  decided it was time to find out about my  lost life I was ready to confront my  past so I sent for my hospital records  and I read them as an experienced  clinician I recognized immediately what  the doctors hadn't been able to see in  1960 my problem wasn't schizophrenia but  incest once I encountered the clinical  reality of my early situation I started  to remember what I had assumed had been  lost forever my memory had not been  destroyed by the shock treatment after  all but had been repressed or  dissociated even before I was  hospitalized I realized I'm a sort of  illustration of  everything that you've heard you know  before this about memory and etc however  confronting the truth of my  circumstances via the records profoundly  retraumatization I developed flashbacks  and nightmares was beset with intrusive  images and became very anxious subject  to emotional flash floods of grief and  despair I lost interest in the world  around me I was focused only on finding  out who I was and what had happened to  me my various perspectives all came  together when I read my hospital records  and discovered the trauma in myself in  the present I had known myself as a  mature seasoned therapist who listened  to her patients with an informed  compassionate understanding of the whole  organic process accepting the painful  ups and downs in the short-term by  holding on to the big picture now I also  knew the immediate pristine existential  perspective of the same woman who  couldn't believe what was happening to  her in her own present life I was caught  off guard when I read my records I had  no idea I could be so unsettled I  thought that I was well analyzed and  prepared to deal with anything life  might bring to me in fact I was pretty  naive throughout this time I continued  to work full-time in my psychology  practice I had my own individual therapy  and the psychologist part of me was  observing and aware of what was  happening to me but the need to pull  myself together  in order to be present and true to my  professional commitment in my office is  an important part of how I survived as  well as I did each day no matter how  dismayed I felt I had to summon my adult  strength so that I could be present for  the people who needed me because I was  working with a skilled and experienced  therapist in my own psychotherapy and  recovery is an orderly process with time  I emerged from the other side my  symptoms receded  and I regained an interest in the world  outside myself some of the emotional  intensity of my experience even though  it was relatively recent disappeared  and as with childbirth or a terrible  nightmare only the intellectual  awareness that I experienced some  feelings beyond ordinary comprehension  remains the essence of my interest in  describing these things to you is to  emphasize that we always have to pay  mindful attention what happened to me  initially was a failure to pay attention  and what ultimately enabled me to  recover each time came from informed  careful exquisitely personal attention  the diagnosis and treatments may change  today it may be borderline or bipolar  rather than schizophrenia and the  treatment may be powerful pills  rather than shock treatment but with  today's pressures there will always be  the temptation to think that you know  the answer without really looking at the  particular individual in front of you  and to put him or her into the category  at hand because you have the latest  drugs to prescribe or the newest therapy  to apply these seemingly innocent errors  can have devastating consequences and  even if you have the right diagnosis  everyone needs to be known as a whole  unique person no one cures a diagnosis  what my guiding clinicians had in common  was their own self-awareness and  self-confidence  it took paying attention looking me in  the eye and not being afraid to learn  who I was each of them was authentically  him or herself they were real they had  faith in their own ability to act  spontaneously in the moment often the  moment was one of humor and sharing a  play on words or sarcastic observation  about someone else even about me I was  just one of two adults enjoying each  other's company securing that brief  exchange that we were truly  communicating for those seconds I wasn't  alone in the universe unreal and  unattached those  seconds were precious and I remembered  them these therapists also showed that  they cared they won't print off entirely  by am i annoying mannerisms they chose  not to dismiss me as manipulative but to  see the frightened child underneath we  don't do things for no reason I tell my  patients I was a scary and frustrating  patient but I was trying to communicate  the therapists who make a difference  didn't overlook the messages in my  behavior or disregard my tremendous  neediness they were very patient when I  hurt myself they got angry in a way  which communicated that their anger had  to do with their not wanting me to cause  myself more pain and when I got better  they believed in me although it along  with compassion for my pain they  appreciated my strength my intelligence  and my quirky sense of humor and they  each have remained a part of my life  concretely affirming my value as a  healthy adult I didn't have to stay sick  to know them this is one of the most  important aspects of the therapy I  learned from my mentor the late  psychologist Daniel Miller who  philosophically understanding that  defenses are symptoms personal style and  perceptions of the world are rich  sources of information about old  relationships and reflect past and  present strengths this is the essential  model of wellness of respect for  survival and focus on the tools that  make survival and growth possible the  stubbornness that had me relentlessly  pursuing my death that led my earlier  psychiatrists to characterize me as  withholding or resisting shock treatment  is the very same determination that has  led to my professional accomplishments  my writing my book and my speaking here  today as Stanley Heller recently  reminded me what really matters is how  well you are not how sick you are  I didn't suffer from schizophrenia I may  or may not have had a borderline  personality disorder these and several  other diagnoses share many symptoms with  complex post post-traumatic stress  sorter which I did experience twice  however to focus only on symptoms or on  PTSD itself is a trap it's by setting  labels aside by paying attention to the  person right there and holding yourself  open to possibility that you can save  the lives of people like me


